    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Observations of the cosmic microwave background (CMB) have provided a wealth of information about the contents, evolution, and beginnings of the universe. One key component of the CMB is its temperature anisotropies, which support a fundamental assumption of inflation theory: that these fluctuations are quantum-mechanical in origin and have stretched to the size of the observable universe. This leads to the prediction that the phases of the Fourier mode decomposition of the CMB in a co-moving gauge are randomly distributed. Assuming a robust model for reconstructing the inflationary potential, we provide a method for extracting this phase distribution from CMB modes. We further provide preliminary analysis of this distribution.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Amara McCune. (2018). Analysis of the Fourier Mode Phase Distribution in the CMB as a Test of Inflation Theory. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/ns903by9803",
            "type": "preferred citation",
